<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aleutian Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aleutian Islands are a chain of islands extending from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> towards Kamchatka in the North Pacific. In 1942, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> occupied the islands of Attu and Kiska. American forces retook them in 1943.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleutian-islands.jpg" alt="Aleutian Islands" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Schmaltz, NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aleutian_Islands_amo_2014135_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Aleutian Islands are a chain of 14 main volcanic islands and 55 smaller ones. They extend westward roughly 1,200 miles (1,900 km) from the Alaska Peninsula towards Kamchatka and separate the Bering Sea to the north from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> to the south. The 57 volcanoes of the islands form the northernmost part of the Pacific Ring of Fire. Before 1867 the islands were known as the Catherine Archipelago. The climate is oceanic, with heavy rainfall and almost constant fog, and the vast majority of the chain has no native trees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first occupants probably arrived following Beringian migrations. The earliest archaeological sites, on Unalaska, date to about 9,000 years ago, those on Adak to about 6,000 years ago, and the western islands were settled last, about 2,500 years ago. The native people call themselves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleut">Unangan</a> and are generally known as Aleut. They developed a culture tied to marine resources, with boat building, tools for fishing and sea-mammal hunting, and noted basketry and weaving.</p><p>In 1741 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitus-bering">Vitus Bering</a> and Aleksei Chirikov sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on a voyage of discovery, and after a storm separated them Chirikov found several eastern islands and Bering several western ones. Bering was shipwrecked and died on the Commander Islands, and his survivors reported that the islands were rich in fur-bearing animals. Siberian fur hunters moved eastward across the chain, and the islands belonged to Russia until the Alaska Purchase of 1867. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigory-shelikhov">Grigory Shelikhov</a> founded a company in the 1780s based on the systematic exploitation of the indigenous peoples, with sporadic conflict such as the Awa'uq Massacre. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> missionaries arrived in 1793.</p><p>On 3 June 1942 Japanese forces invaded Attu and Kiska, the only invasions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> during the war. The United States moved over 800 civilians to camps in the Alaska Panhandle, and more than 90 Americans were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as prisoners. American forces landed on Attu in May 1943 and on Kiska later that year, where 313 Allied men died from accidents. The United States tested nuclear weapons underground on Amchitka from 1965 to 1971.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Most of the islands belong to the U.S. state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>, while the Commander Islands, farther west, belong to Kamchatka Krai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 7,152 people on the islands, 4,254 of them in Unalaska, the main settlement. Fishing is the main economic activity, and nearly all the islands are protected within the Alaska Maritime National Wildlife Refuge. Large seabird colonies breed there, and sea otters help keep kelp forests along the coasts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aleutian Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By mid-1942, following the fall of Singapore in February, the conquest of the Dutch East Indies, and the extension of the Pacific perimeter to the <b>Aleutian Islands</b>, Japan controlled approximately seven million square kilometres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the same period the use of polished slate and Thule methods of making pottery spread west to the <b>Aleutian Islands</b> and southern Alaska.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thule-people">Thule people · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>After Vitus Bering's voyage of 1741, Siberian traders hunted sea otters in the <b>Aleutian Islands</b>, and Grigory Shelikhov founded a permanent settlement on Kodiak Island in 1784.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
